{
  "meta": {
    "title": "RA-05 — Open Loyalty capability: specification-based desk pass (live-instance spike NOT run)",
    "studyType": "Desk / secondary research — API specification and vendor-documentation analysis. This is NOT the technical spike RA-05 calls for. RA-05's trigger is 'access to a real Open Loyalty instance, not the in-repo mock (RAID D005)' and that access does not exist. Nothing below is verified engine behaviour: the OpenAPI spec gives shapes, not semantics. Every verdict is a reading of documents and must be confirmed against a live tenant before any member-facing design commits to it. The live-instance spike remains OPEN.",
    "cycle": "NTUC Club Member PWA • Discovery cycle 1 • Week 1",
    "researcher": "Gen-e2 Research Agent (autonomous desk pass) — human review required before this is treated as validated input",
    "lastUpdated": "2026-08-17",
    "summary": "Reading the vendored 195-path OpenAPI spec against the five member-facing behaviours in RA-05's success criteria: refund reversal and voucher-wallet semantics are expressible in the engine with configuration; expiry-cohort visibility exists but only on a deprecated endpoint whose payload shape is undocumented; and two of the five things RA-05 asks about — multiple redemptions in one transaction (BR-P1-017) and two of the five BR-P1-002b notification triggers (birthday, lapsed visit) — have no engine mechanism at all and fall entirely to the integration layer as unbudgeted member-surface work."
  },
  "groups": [
    {
      "id": "capability-verdicts",
      "title": "Capability Verdicts",
      "description": "One insight per member-facing behaviour named in RA-05's success criteria, each rated native / native-with-configuration / integration-layer-work-required. Ratings describe what the specification and the vendor's public documentation permit, not what a running instance does. Confidence is tagged on every insight using the standard framework; nothing here is tagged 'validated' because no engine was exercised.",
      "insights": [
        {
          "id": "verdict-expiry-cohorts",
          "title": "Expiry-cohort visibility is NATIVE WITH CONFIGURATION for the data, but the only ready-made cohort breakdown sits on a deprecated endpoint — deriving it any other way is integration-layer work",
          "summary": "VERDICT: native with configuration (partial) — with a named dependency on a deprecated route. [confidence: strong-signal for what the spec exposes; assumption for whether it is usable] Three separate mechanisms bear on 'which points lapse when'. (1) The 12-month rolling window of BR-P1-011 is configurable per wallet type: `PostWalletTypeRequestBody.walletType.unitDaysExpiryAfter` is an enum of `all_time_active | after_x_days | at_the_end_of_the_month | at_the_end_of_the_x_th_year | at_the_end_of_the_chosen_date`, with `unitDaysActiveCount` for the day count — so `after_x_days: 365` satisfies BR-P1-011's 'configurable' clause natively. (2) Per-cohort raw data is on the ledger: `components.schemas.Transfer` carries `expiresAt`, and the member-token route `GET /api/{storeCode}/member/points` (labelled Integration, not deprecated) is both filterable and `_orderBy`-sortable on `expiresAt`. (3) A pre-computed breakdown exists — `CustomerStatus.pointsExpiringBreakdown` (an object of `additionalProperties: {type: number}`) plus `pointsExpiringNextMonth` — and this is exactly the shape f4 needs. THE PROBLEM: `CustomerStatus` is referenced by exactly one path in the whole 195-path spec, `GET /api/{storeCode}/member/{member}/status`, and that path carries `deprecated: true` with no replacement anywhere in the document. Open Loyalty's own public API reference confirms the deprecation and does not name a successor, and does not document the key format of the breakdown map. SECOND PROBLEM: deriving cohorts from `/member/points` instead is not a simple read. `Transfer` exposes `value` but no remaining or consumed amount; spends and expiries are separate transfer rows. To state 'you have 300 points lapsing on 3 March' the integration layer must replicate the engine's consumption order — and neither the OpenAPI spec nor Open Loyalty's public wallet documentation states whether spending consumes FIFO, nearest-expiry, or something else. This is the single most load-bearing undocumented semantic in the whole desk pass.",
