        {
          "id": "verdict-multi-redemption",
          "title": "Multiple redemptions in one transaction (BR-P1-017) is INTEGRATION-LAYER WORK REQUIRED — the engine has no concept of a transaction that groups coupon usages",
          "summary": "VERDICT: integration-layer work required. ESCALATE. [confidence: strong-signal] Three independent readings converge. (1) `components.schemas.RedeemCouponFormType` is `{ required: [couponCode], properties: { couponCode: string } }` — singular, required, one coupon per call. Open Loyalty's public API reference for 'Mark coupon as used' independently confirms one code per request and describes no bulk mechanism. (2) `components.schemas.PostTransaction` has no coupon, voucher or discount field anywhere in it — `header`, `items`, `customerData`, `channelId`, and nothing else. The engine's transaction model therefore cannot record that two vouchers were consumed against one sale, which means it cannot answer 'what did the member redeem on this bill' and cannot reconcile it for BR-P1-015 funding attribution either. (3) `POST /api/{storeCode}/redemption/status/bulk` is the only bulk endpoint in the reward domain, and it is the wrong one: it moves issued rewards through the fulfilment lifecycle (`issued … completed`), not coupon usage, and its own description states that per-item constraint violations are 'accepted and that item is reported as failed in the response' — explicitly non-atomic. CONSEQUENCE: stacking N vouchers at one till is N sequential `redeem` calls with no engine-side grouping, no atomicity and no rollback. Everything the member sees about a multi-voucher bill, and every partial-failure recovery when voucher 2 of 3 fails after voucher 1 has already been burned, is PALO IT's to design and build on both the member and merchant surfaces. A hold-then-commit pattern is conceivable — `POST /api/{storeCode}/points/block` and `POST /api/{storeCode}/points/{transfer}/cancel` exist — but no such pattern is documented for coupons, and `/points/block` operates on units, not vouchers. [that last sentence: hypothesis]",
          "references": [
            {
              "id": "multi-ref-1",
              "sourceId": "SPEC",
              "filePath": "spec/openloyalty-openapi.json",
              "range": { "start": { "line": 0, "character": 0 }, "end": { "line": 0, "character": 0 } },
