        {
          "id": "verdict-refund-reversal",
          "title": "Refund reversal is NATIVE WITH CONFIGURATION for the points, but the engine will not revert tier, will not flag the already-spent case, and will not tell the member which purchase was reversed",
          "summary": "VERDICT: native with configuration for the reversal itself; integration-layer work for all three of BR-P1-012's surrounding obligations. [confidence: strong-signal for the mechanism, corroborated by vendor docs; assumption for the already-spent behaviour, which no source states] The mechanism is real: `PostTransaction.transaction.header.documentType` is an enum of `sell | return` with `linkedDocumentNumber` tying a return to its original, and `components.schemas.Rule.effects.effect` includes `cancel_transaction_effects`, paired with the `return_transaction` value in the campaign `trigger` enum. But reversal is NOT automatic — it requires an operator to have configured a campaign on the `return_transaction` trigger, and Open Loyalty's public campaign documentation adds two operational conditions the spec omits: the return must be matched to an existing transaction, and items must be added to the return transaction manually. GAP A — tier does not revert. The spec's own `Rule` description says it outright: 'cancel_transaction_effects reverses the points the original transaction earned and leaves the tier standing.' The vendor docs agree that returns 'do not revert the member's status or tier … nor adjust campaign limits or counters'. Commercially defensible, but the member surface must never claim tier standing reflects net spend, and CX needs an answer for the member who notices. GAP B — BR-P1-012's second clause has no home. The BRD requires the platform to 'flag for manual adjustment where the points have already been redeemed'. The engine's only lever is `WalletType.allowNegativeBalance`: either the balance goes negative, or (spec and docs both silent) the deduction fails. Neither is a flag, neither raises a case, neither is visible to CX. The exception queue, the CX workflow and the member-facing explanation are all integration-layer and service-design work. Admin remediation exists (`POST /api/{storeCode}/bulkAction/{type}` with `cancelTransfers` / `changeExpirationTransfers`, and `POST /api/{storeCode}/points/{transfer}/cancel`) but nothing in the engine surfaces the exception in the first place. GAP C — the reversal is unexplainable to the member without extra calls. A reversal lands in `GET /api/{storeCode}/member/{member}/history` as a `PointsWereCanceled` entry whose `flatVariables` carry `transferId`, `points`, `comment` and `activePoints` and NO `actionCause`. To say 'this reversal relates to your 3 March purchase at Tenant X' the integration layer must join each history row to `GET /api/{storeCode}/points/{transfer}` (which does carry `actionCause.transactionId`) and then to the transaction. Two extra round-trips per explained ledger row — directly against feature f4 and guiding principle 2, 'never show a member a number they cannot explain'.",
          "references": [
            {
              "id": "refund-ref-1",
              "sourceId": "SPEC",
              "filePath": "spec/openloyalty-openapi.json",
              "range": { "start": { "line": 0, "character": 0 }, "end": { "line": 0, "character": 0 } },
              "note": "Spec path components.schemas.Rule.items.properties.effects.items.properties.effect.description — the engine documenting its own tier behaviour on a return. This is the sentence that decides Gap A.",
              "excerpt": "\"A returned transaction does not take the tier back. `cancel_transaction_effects` reverses the points the original transaction earned and leaves the tier standing, along with the mark that protects it from an automatic downgrade.\""
            },
            {
              "id": "refund-ref-2",
              "sourceId": "SPEC",
              "filePath": "spec/openloyalty-openapi.json",
              "range": { "start": { "line": 0, "character": 0 }, "end": { "line": 0, "character": 0 } },
              "note": "Spec paths components.schemas.PostTransaction.transaction.header and components.schemas.CampaignRequestBody.campaign.trigger — returns are first-class and there is a trigger for them, so the mechanism exists and only needs configuring.",
              "excerpt": "\"documentType\": {\"enum\": [\"sell\", \"return\"]}, \"linkedDocumentNumber\": {\"description\": \"Used only for return transactions and refers to the original 'sell' document.\"} … \"trigger\": {\"enum\": [\"transaction\", \"return_transaction\", \"custom_event\", \"internal_event\", \"time\", \"achievement\", \"custom_event_unique_code\"]}"
            },
            {
              "id": "refund-ref-3",
              "sourceId": "SPEC",
              "filePath": "spec/openloyalty-openapi.json",
              "range": { "start": { "line": 0, "character": 0 }, "end": { "line": 0, "character": 0 } },
              "note": "Spec path components.schemas.PointsWereCanceled.flatVariables — the complete set. The absence of actionCause is Gap C: the member ledger cannot self-explain a reversal.",
              "excerpt": "\"required\": [\"transferId\", \"walletId\", \"walletTypeCode\", \"walletUnitSingularName\", \"walletUnitPluralName\", \"points\", \"activePoints\"] — no actionCause property present"
            },
            {
              "id": "refund-ref-4",
              "sourceId": "SPEC",
              "filePath": "spec/openloyalty-openapi.json",
              "range": { "start": { "line": 0, "character": 0 }, "end": { "line": 0, "character": 0 } },
              "note": "Spec path components.schemas.PostWalletTypeRequestBody.walletType.allowNegativeBalance — the engine's only response to a reversal that exceeds the balance. It is a boolean, not a flag or a case.",
              "excerpt": "\"allowNegativeBalance\": {\"description\": \"Indicates if negative balance is allowed\", \"type\": \"boolean\", \"default\": false}"
            },
            {
              "id": "refund-ref-5",
              "sourceId": "DOC",
              "filePath": "gen-e2/discover/customer-app/01-research/findings/RA-05-openloyalty-capability-desk-pass.gen-e2.research",
              "range": { "start": { "line": 0, "character": 0 }, "end": { "line": 0, "character": 0 } },
              "note": "Desk research: Open Loyalty public help centre, 'Return Transaction' campaign trigger (https://help.openloyalty.io/main-features/campaigns/campaigns-and-referral-campaigns/creating-campaigns/trigger-types/return-transaction), accessed 2026-08-17. Independently corroborates that reversal is campaign-driven and that tier and campaign counters are untouched — two sources agreeing, which is why this insight is strong-signal rather than assumption.",
              "excerpt": "\"Return transaction has to be matched with an existing transaction. Items must be manually added to the return transaction.\" … \"do not revert the member's status or tier if it was affected by the original transaction, nor adjust campaign limits or counters\""
            },
            {
              "id": "refund-ref-6",
              "sourceId": "BRD",
              "filePath": "gen-e2/discover/general-references/extracted/tender-BRD-requirements.txt",
              "range": { "start": { "line": 409, "character": 0 }, "end": { "line": 413, "character": 0 } },
              "note": "BR-P1-012, Must Have. Its three clauses map to three different verdicts: automatic reversal = configurable, auditable ledger = native, flag-for-manual-adjustment = nothing.",
              "excerpt": "\"the platform must automatically reverse the associated points (or flag for manual adjustment where the points have already been redeemed), maintaining an auditable ledger.\""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "verdict-webhook-coverage",
          "title": "Webhooks cover three of the five BR-P1-002b notification triggers natively; birthday and lapsed visit have no engine event and are INTEGRATION-LAYER WORK REQUIRED",
          "summary": "VERDICT: 3 native / 2 integration-layer work required. ESCALATE the two. [confidence: strong-signal — the spec's x-webhooks block and Open Loyalty's public 'What triggers a webhook' page enumerate the same set] The engine publishes 31 webhook events under `x-webhooks`, HMAC-signed (`x-webhook-hmac-signing`), each carrying a `messageId` for de-duplication, with one retry on a 4xx/5xx and — worth noting for availability — synchronous delivery that waits for the receiver's response. Mapping BR-P1-002b's five named campaign triggers: ENROLMENT → `CustomerRegistered` and `CustomerWasRegisteredWithoutActivation`, native. TIER UPGRADE → `CustomerLevelChanged`, native, and its payload carries `level.levelMoveUp` / `levelMoveDown` so an upgrade is distinguishable from a downgrade without inference. POINTS EXPIRY → `PointsWillExpire`, native with configuration; the payload carries `points`, `remainingUnits` and `pointsWillExpire`, and the vendor docs state it is 'sent X days before units expire' per an expiring-notifications setting — a setting that appears nowhere in the OpenAPI, because `GET /api/{storeCode}/settings` returns an untyped `additionalProperties: string` map. BIRTHDAY → no webhook exists. Two configurations get close and neither produces a semantically named event: a time-triggered campaign can use `TriggerStrategy.type: birthday`, but it emits the generic `CampaignEffectWasApplied`, which the Notification Service must decode by campaign id; and BR-P1-024's actual mechanic — a multiplier on the FIRST QUALIFYING TRANSACTION in the birthday month — is not the `birthday` trigger strategy at all but a `transaction`-triggered campaign with a date condition (`is_month_of_year` is in the operator enum and `birthDate` is a member field). Two different campaigns, both emitting the same anonymous effect event. LAPSED VISIT → no webhook, and no usable substitute. Segments can express lapsing perfectly well (the segment condition enum includes `last_purchase_n_days_before`, `last_transaction_between_days`, `purchase_period`), but the only segment webhook is `SegmentMembershipDropped`, and its payload is `WebhookSegment { segmentId, segmentName, previousCount, newCount }` — it does not name a member, and it fires on leaving a segment, not joining one. There is no per-member 'became lapsed' event anywhere in the 31. Origination falls to scheduled polling of `GET /api/{storeCode}/segment/{segment}/members` with local diffing and de-duplication.",
          "references": [
            {
              "id": "webhook-ref-1",
              "sourceId": "SPEC",
              "filePath": "spec/openloyalty-openapi.json",
              "range": { "start": { "line": 0, "character": 0 }, "end": { "line": 0, "character": 0 } },
              "note": "Spec block x-webhooks — the full enumerated set of 31 event types. Read it as the complete list: neither a birthday event nor a per-member lapsed/inactivity event appears anywhere in it.",
              "excerpt": "AchievementCreated, AchievementUpdated, CampaignCreated, CampaignUpdated, AvailablePointsAmountChanged, CouponWillExpire, CustomerBoughtReward, CustomerDeactivated, CustomerEmailWasChanged, CustomerLevelChanged, CustomerPhoneNumberWasChanged, CustomerRequestedPasswordReset, CustomerRequestedSendActivationCode, CustomerUpdated, CustomerWasRegisteredWithoutActivation, TransactionAssignedToCustomer, LevelWillExpire, PointsWillExpire, CustomerRegistered, TransactionRegistered, RewardRedemptionStatusChanged, RewardCreated, RewardUpdated, CampaignEffectWasApplied, MemberAchievementProgressWasChanged, MemberChallengeProgressWasChanged, UnitsTransferWasAdded, AchievementsWereProcessed, SegmentMembershipDropped, ChallengesWereProcessed, WalletBalanceUpdated"
            },
            {
