    {
      "id": "escalation",
      "title": "Escalation — Unbudgeted Member-Surface Engineering",
      "description": "RA-05's success criteria state: 'Anything in the integration-layer column is unbudgeted member-surface work and must be escalated the day it is found.' This group is that escalation, found 2026-08-17. Effort shapes are the agent's estimate of the SHAPE of the work — which components, which failure paths, which surfaces — not a sizing. Sizing requires an engineer with the delivery plan and is the human's to produce. All items are conditional on the live-instance spike confirming the desk reading.",
      "insights": [
        {
          "id": "escalate-multi-redemption",
          "title": "E1 — Multi-voucher redemption at a till must be orchestrated end to end by PALO IT, including the partial-failure path, and it touches both the member and merchant surfaces",
          "summary": "HIGHEST PRIORITY ESCALATION. [confidence: strong-signal] BR-P1-017 is a Must Have with no engine mechanism. Shape of the work: (1) an integration-layer redemption orchestrator that accepts a set of coupon codes and calls `redeem` N times; (2) a compensating-action path using `.../reward/reissue` for when voucher k fails after vouchers 1..k-1 have been burned — reissue exists, so recovery is possible, but the decision logic, the idempotency keys and the audit trail are ours; (3) a member-facing model for 'these vouchers went on one bill' that the engine cannot store, which means PALO IT owns that record and its reconciliation against BR-P1-015 funding attribution; (4) a merchant-surface flow for staff scanning several vouchers in sequence with a queue behind them; (5) a rule for whether vouchers may be combined at all — a stacking policy the BRD does not state and the engine cannot enforce. This is not one component. It spans the member app, the merchant scanner, the integration layer and a reconciliation record, and every one of them has a peak-hour failure path. It also sits directly on top of the unresolved question in problem signal s5 about who initiates redemption — which means it cannot be scoped before RA-03 observes the till.",
          "references": [
            {
              "id": "esc-multi-ref-1",
              "sourceId": "PLAN",
              "filePath": "gen-e2/discover/customer-app/01-research/02-research-plan.md",
              "range": { "start": { "line": 137, "character": 0 }, "end": { "line": 148, "character": 0 } },
              "note": "The RA-05 entry's own escalation instruction, which this group discharges.",
              "excerpt": "\"Anything in the 'integration-layer' column is unbudgeted member-surface work and must be escalated the day it is found.\""
            },
            {
              "id": "esc-multi-ref-2",
              "sourceId": "PLAN",
              "filePath": "gen-e2/discover/customer-app/01-research/02-research-plan.md",
              "range": { "start": { "line": 225, "character": 0 }, "end": { "line": 227, "character": 0 } },
              "note": "The sequencing note predicted exactly this class of finding and named multi-redemption as one of three things to check. It is the one that came back worst.",
              "excerpt": "\"If Open Loyalty cannot natively express the expiry horizon, the voucher wallet or multi-redemption, that is unbudgeted member-surface engineering discovered at week 1 rather than week 12.\""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "escalate-notification-origination",
          "title": "E2 — The Notification Service as architected cannot originate the birthday or lapsed-visit campaigns, because it is webhook-driven and neither trigger emits a webhook",
          "summary": "SECOND PRIORITY ESCALATION, and a direct architectural contradiction. [confidence: strong-signal] ⚠️ CONTRADICTION: the solutioning deck defines the Notification Service as 'An API service that accepts webhook requests from OpenLoyalty and creates notifications that are sent to the marketing platform', and claims the platform provides 'outbound webhooks on every member and ledger mutation'. Both statements are true and neither is sufficient: birthday and lapsed visit are not member or ledger mutations, so no webhook exists for them, so a purely webhook-driven Notification Service cannot originate two of the five BR-P1-002b triggers. Shape of the work: a scheduler alongside the webhook receiver; for lapsed visit, periodic polling of `GET /api/{storeCode}/segment/{segment}/members` for a lapsed segment, diffed against local state to detect entry, with de-duplication so a member is not re-notified every poll; for birthday, either the same scheduler reading `birthDate` off the member record, or a `CampaignEffectWasApplied` subscription plus a campaign-id-to-semantic-event mapping table that the Campaign Studio and the Notification Service both have to agree on and keep in sync. The second option couples marketing configuration to notification code, which is the kind of coupling BRD NFR-011/NFR-012 exist to prevent. Both options add state, a scheduler and a failure mode (missed or duplicated notifications) to a service the deck draws as stateless.",
          "references": [
            {
              "id": "esc-notif-ref-1",
              "sourceId": "SOLUTIONING",
              "filePath": "gen-e2/discover/general-references/extracted/solutioning.txt",
              "range": { "start": { "line": 252, "character": 0 }, "end": { "line": 255, "character": 0 } },
              "note": "The Notification Service definition. Webhook-in, notification-out, no scheduler. The contradiction is structural, not a wording problem.",
              "excerpt": "\"Notification Service — An API service that accepts webhook requests from OpenLoyalty and creates notifications that are sent to the marketing platform.\""
            },
            {
              "id": "esc-notif-ref-2",
              "sourceId": "SOLUTIONING",
              "filePath": "gen-e2/discover/general-references/extracted/solutioning.txt",
              "range": { "start": { "line": 204, "character": 0 }, "end": { "line": 207, "character": 0 } },
              "note": "The capability claim made to the client. Accurate as far as it goes; the gap is that two BRD-named triggers are outside the class of events it describes.",
              "excerpt": "\"Outbound webhooks on every member and ledger mutation\""
            },
            {
              "id": "esc-notif-ref-3",
              "sourceId": "BRIEF",
              "filePath": "gen-e2/discover/customer-app/ntuc-member-pwa.gen-e2.brief",
              "range": { "start": { "line": 161, "character": 0 }, "end": { "line": 161, "character": 0 } },
              "note": "Feature f11 promises the member enrolment, tier-upgrade, birthday and lapsed-visit notifications as MVP. Two of the four have no engine event behind them.",
              "excerpt": "\"Member notification ahead of points expiry, plus enrolment, tier-upgrade, birthday and lapsed-visit triggers…\""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "escalate-expiry-derivation",
          "title": "E3 — If the deprecated status endpoint is withdrawn or its breakdown proves unusable, PALO IT must derive expiry cohorts itself — and cannot, without knowing the engine's consumption order",
          "summary": "CONDITIONAL ESCALATION — resolve at T1/T2 before designing f4. [confidence: assumption] Two outcomes, very different costs. If `GET /api/{storeCode}/member/{member}/status` still returns a usable `pointsExpiringBreakdown` on the live tenant, f4's expiry horizon is a read and the cost is a deprecation risk to carry in RAID. If it does not — withdrawn, empty, or keyed in a format that cannot be rendered to a member — the integration layer must compute cohorts from `/member/points` by pairing spend transfers against earning transfers, which requires knowing whether the engine consumes FIFO or nearest-expiry. Neither the OpenAPI nor the vendor's wallet documentation states it, and getting it wrong means showing members a lapse warning for points they have already spent, or hiding one for points they still hold. That is a trust failure at exactly the moment guiding principle 2 says trust is won or lost. Shape of the work in the bad case: a shadow ledger in the integration layer, reconciled against `UnitsTransferWasAdded` and `WalletBalanceUpdated` webhooks — both of which carry enough to maintain it (`expiresAt`, `type`, `relatedTransferId` for expired transfers, and a `reason` enum of `added|spent|expired|activated|cancelled|blocked|unblocked`) — plus a reconciliation job and a discrepancy alarm. A shadow ledger of the loyalty balance is a serious piece of engineering to discover at week 1 and a severe one to discover at week 12.",
          "references": [
            {
              "id": "esc-expiry-ref-1",
              "sourceId": "SPEC",
              "filePath": "spec/openloyalty-openapi.json",
              "range": { "start": { "line": 0, "character": 0 }, "end": { "line": 0, "character": 0 } },
              "note": "Spec block x-webhooks.WalletBalanceUpdated and x-webhooks.UnitsTransferWasAdded — these are what a shadow ledger would be built on, and they are rich enough to make it feasible. Feasible is not the same as budgeted.",
              "excerpt": "\"reason\": {\"enum\": [\"added\", \"spent\", \"expired\", \"activated\", \"cancelled\", \"blocked\", \"unblocked\"]} … \"relatedTransferId\": {\"description\": \"Only for transfers with type 'expired'\"}"
            },
            {
              "id": "esc-expiry-ref-2",
              "sourceId": "BRIEF",
              "filePath": "gen-e2/discover/customer-app/ntuc-member-pwa.gen-e2.brief",
              "range": { "start": { "line": 154, "character": 0 }, "end": { "line": 154, "character": 0 } },
              "note": "Feature f4 promises the member 'which points lapse when'. Whether that promise is a read or a shadow ledger is decided by one call against a live instance.",
              "excerpt": "\"shows which points lapse when against the 12-month horizon\""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "escalate-refund-exception-and-ledger",
          "title": "E4 — The refund exception queue, the tier-stays-put explanation and the per-row ledger join are three separate pieces of member-surface work sitting behind one BRD line",
          "summary": "THIRD PRIORITY ESCALATION. [confidence: strong-signal for the ledger join; assumption for the exception behaviour until T4 runs] Shape of the work, in three parts. (a) BR-P1-012's 'flag for manual adjustment' has no engine equivalent: PALO IT must detect the already-spent case (either by pre-checking the balance before posting the return, or by catching a negative balance after), raise a case, route it to CX, and decide what the member sees in the meantime — a negative balance shown to a member with no explanation is the worst possible outcome and is the default if nothing is built. (b) The tier does not revert on a refund, by design and by the engine's own documentation, so both the member surface and the CX script need a defensible account of why status survived a reversed purchase. (c) Every explained ledger row costs two extra calls, because `PointsWereCanceled` and its siblings carry no `actionCause` — so f4's 'states plainly when a refund has reversed an earning' is an N+1 join per row, needing caching or a denormalised local history to stay inside the response-time target of success metric m5. None of the three is large alone. Together they are the difference between f4 as a list and f4 as the trust instrument guiding principle 2 requires.",
          "references": [
            {
              "id": "esc-refund-ref-1",
              "sourceId": "BRIEF",
              "filePath": "gen-e2/discover/customer-app/ntuc-member-pwa.gen-e2.brief",
              "range": { "start": { "line": 93, "character": 0 }, "end": { "line": 99, "character": 0 } },
              "note": "Problem signal s4 framed expiry and reversal as trust events handled as ledger rows. The desk pass confirms the engine hands us ledger rows and nothing else — the trust layer is entirely ours.",
              "excerpt": "\"These are the two moments where a member's balance goes down through no action of their own.\""
            },
            {
              "id": "esc-refund-ref-2",
              "sourceId": "BRIEF",
              "filePath": "gen-e2/discover/customer-app/ntuc-member-pwa.gen-e2.brief",
              "range": { "start": { "line": 215, "character": 0 }, "end": { "line": 215, "character": 0 } },
              "note": "Guiding principle 2 is the standard this work is measured against, and it is the principle the engine's flat history rows cannot meet unaided.",
              "excerpt": "\"Never show a member a number they cannot explain\""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "escalate-brokering-and-qr",
          "title": "E5 — Every member-initiated action must be brokered by the integration layer with an admin credential, because the engine labels buying and redeeming as Management operations",
          "summary": "LOWER PRIORITY BUT ARCHITECTURALLY LOAD-BEARING. [confidence: assumption — the spec's scope labels are documentation, not enforcement, and T7 must confirm them] `POST /api/{storeCode}/reward/{reward}/buy` is labelled Management and its request bodies (`StaticCoupon`, `DynamicCoupon`, `ConversionCoupon`) all require `customerId` in the body rather than deriving it from the caller — the shape of an administrator acting on a member's behalf, not a member acting for themselves. The same pattern holds for `POST /points/spend`. Combined with the deprecation of `GET /member/reward/bought`, this means the member-driven rewards flow the current PWA implements has no supported member-token path in the engine: catalogue browse, buy, and voucher wallet must all be brokered server-side with an admin credential, with PALO IT enforcing that member A cannot act on member B. That is standard for a headless deployment and consistent with the brief's 'the engine is headless and stays that way', but it should be a stated architectural position rather than an accident, and it has a security consequence — a long-lived admin credential in the integration layer, which the BRD's ISO 27001 / SOC 2 obligations will be read against. Add to this the small but real item that the engine issues `issuedCoupon.code` as a bare string: QR symbology, rendering, and offline presentation of both the membership card and the voucher (features f1, f6, f12, guiding principle 1) are entirely member-surface work with no engine support.",
          "references": [
            {
              "id": "esc-broker-ref-1",
              "sourceId": "SPEC",
              "filePath": "spec/openloyalty-openapi.json",
              "range": { "start": { "line": 0, "character": 0 }, "end": { "line": 0, "character": 0 } },
              "note": "Spec paths ./api/{storeCode}/reward/{reward}/buy.post (Management label) and components.schemas.ConversionCoupon — customerId is required in the body, which is the signature of an admin-scoped operation.",
              "excerpt": "\"Management\" … ConversionCoupon: {\"required\": [\"customerId\"], \"properties\": {\"customerId\": {…}, \"dateValid\": {…}, \"units\": {…}}}"
            },
            {
              "id": "esc-broker-ref-2",
              "sourceId": "BRIEF",
              "filePath": "gen-e2/discover/customer-app/ntuc-member-pwa.gen-e2.brief",
              "range": { "start": { "line": 226, "character": 0 }, "end": { "line": 226, "character": 0 } },
              "note": "The brief already flags the repository's localStorage token pattern as not production-acceptable. A brokered architecture with a server-held admin credential is the answer, but it needs stating and securing deliberately.",
              "excerpt": "\"the repository's current pattern of holding the OpenLoyalty token in localStorage is explicitly flagged as dev-grade and is not production-acceptable\""
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "test-first",
      "title": "Test This First — Ordered Calls for the Live Instance",
      "description": "The specific calls to make the moment a real Open Loyalty tenant is available (RAID D005), ordered by how much unbudgeted work hangs on the answer rather than by how easy they are to run. Each states what would confirm the desk verdict and what would break it. Running T1–T5 is the minimum that turns this desk pass into the spike RA-05 actually asks for.",
      "insights": [
        {
          "id": "test-t1-expiry-breakdown",
          "title": "T1 — Call the deprecated status endpoint and find out whether pointsExpiringBreakdown still works and what its keys look like",
          "summary": "STAKES: the entire cost of feature f4's expiry horizon — a read, or a shadow ledger (E3). CALL: `GET /api/{storeCode}/member/{member}/status`, with an admin token and again with a member JWT. LOOK FOR: does it 200 at all; is `pointsExpiringBreakdown` populated for a member holding points with several different `expiresAt` values; what is the key format (ISO date? `YYYY-MM`? day count?); does the member's own JWT get through, or only an admin's. CONFIRMS THE VERDICT IF: it returns a dated map that can be rendered to a member. BREAKS IT IF: the field is absent, empty, or keyed in something unrenderable — at which point E3's shadow ledger becomes live scope and must be escalated the same day. ALSO ASK OPEN LOYALTY DIRECTLY, in the expert-interview half of RA-05: what replaces this endpoint, and on what timeline is the deprecated route removed? [confidence: this is the highest-value single call in the list]",
          "references": [
            {
              "id": "t1-ref-1",
              "sourceId": "RAID",
              "filePath": "gen-e2/discover/customer-app/raid.gen-e2.raid",
              "range": { "start": { "line": 459, "character": 0 }, "end": { "line": 459, "character": 0 } },
              "note": "D005 is the dependency that gates every test in this group. Nothing here can run until it clears.",
              "excerpt": "\"Open Loyalty engine capability confirmation against the real instance — expiry cohorts, voucher wallet semantics, multi-redemption, webhook contract (see R008).\""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "test-t2-consumption-order",
          "title": "T2 — Establish empirically whether spending consumes points FIFO or nearest-expiry, because no document states it",
          "summary": "STAKES: correctness of every expiry warning the member is ever shown (E3). CALL: add three transfers via `POST /api/{storeCode}/points/add` with deliberately different `expiresAt` values and distinguishable amounts; spend an amount that must cross a cohort boundary via `POST /api/{storeCode}/points/spend`; then `GET /api/{storeCode}/member/points?_orderBy[expiresAt]=asc` and read which cohort shrank. Repeat with a spend that exactly exhausts one cohort. LOOK FOR: which earning transfer was drawn down; whether the engine mutates the original transfer's `value` or writes a separate spending row (the schema says the latter, which is why this matters); whether any remaining-amount field appears at runtime that the spec does not document. CONFIRMS: a stable, documentable consumption order. BREAKS: an order that varies, or one that cannot be observed from the member-facing endpoint — either forces E3.",
          "references": [
            {
              "id": "t2-ref-1",
              "sourceId": "SPEC",
              "filePath": "spec/openloyalty-openapi.json",
              "range": { "start": { "line": 0, "character": 0 }, "end": { "line": 0, "character": 0 } },
              "note": "Spec path paths./api/{storeCode}/member/points.get — the endpoint under test, and the one the member surface would actually use. Its expiresAt filter and _orderBy support are what make the experiment readable.",
              "excerpt": "\"name\": \"expiresAt\", \"in\": \"query\", \"schema\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"format\": \"date-time\"}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "test-t3-multi-redeem",
          "title": "T3 — Burn two coupons in quick succession and characterise the failure path, because BR-P1-017 has to be built on top of whatever it does",
          "summary": "STAKES: the design of E1, the largest escalation in this pass. CALL: issue two coupons to one member, then `POST /api/{storeCode}/member/{member}/reward/redeem` twice in rapid succession; then attempt to redeem an already-used code; then an expired one; then attempt `.../reward/reissue` on a used code and re-redeem it. LOOK FOR: the exact status and body on each failure — the spec advertises 400, 404 and 409 but describes none of them; whether the second call while the first is in flight 409s (the spec's Conflict response is described as 'the resource is already being processed by another concurrent request', which suggests per-resource locking, not per-member); whether `reissue` fully restores a coupon or leaves a trace; whether anything anywhere in the engine now links the two redemptions. CONFIRMS: reissue is a usable compensating action, which makes the orchestrator tractable. BREAKS: reissue is lossy or refused after use — in which case a partial multi-voucher failure is unrecoverable in the engine and the member is owed a manual remedy, which changes both the CX model and the sizing of E1.",
          "references": [
            {
              "id": "t3-ref-1",
              "sourceId": "SPEC",
              "filePath": "spec/openloyalty-openapi.json",
              "range": { "start": { "line": 0, "character": 0 }, "end": { "line": 0, "character": 0 } },
              "note": "Spec path paths./api/{storeCode}/member/{member}/reward/redeem.post.responses — a 409 is advertised but never explained. The semantics of that conflict decide whether sequential redemption is safe under a queue at peak.",
              "excerpt": "\"400\": BadRequest, \"404\": NotFound, \"409\": Conflict — no description of which condition produces which"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "test-t4-refund-paths",
          "title": "T4 — Post a partial return against a spent balance and find out what the engine actually does, because BR-P1-012's hardest clause depends on it",
          "summary": "STAKES: whether E4's exception queue is a small guard or a full CX workflow. CALL: configure a campaign with `trigger: return_transaction` and the `cancel_transaction_effects` effect. Post a `sell` transaction, let points land, then post a `return` with `linkedDocumentNumber` set and only SOME of the original items. Then repeat the whole sequence but spend the points first, with `allowNegativeBalance: false`, and again with it `true`. LOOK FOR: on a partial return, are points reversed proportionally to the returned items or in full; with a spent balance and negative balance disallowed, does the reversal fail loudly, fail silently, or partially apply; what appears in `GET /member/{member}/history` in each case; does the tier really stay put as the spec claims; is anything at all raised that CX could see. CONFIRMS: proportional reversal plus a clean, detectable failure on the spent case. BREAKS: a silent partial application — the worst outcome, because the member's balance would then be wrong with no signal to anyone.",
          "references": [
            {
              "id": "t4-ref-1",
              "sourceId": "BRD",
              "filePath": "gen-e2/discover/general-references/extracted/tender-BRD-requirements.txt",
              "range": { "start": { "line": 409, "character": 0 }, "end": { "line": 413, "character": 0 } },
              "note": "BR-P1-012 again — the clause 'or flag for manual adjustment where the points have already been redeemed' is the specific thing this test exists to price.",
              "excerpt": "\"(or flag for manual adjustment where the points have already been redeemed), maintaining an auditable ledger\""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "test-t5-webhook-reality",
          "title": "T5 — Ask the instance for its own webhook event list and subscribe to everything, because the spec's list may not be the tenant's list",
          "summary": "STAKES: whether E2 is two triggers or more. CALL: `GET /api/{storeCode}/webhook/event` — which in the spec returns nothing more than `items: [string]`, so the real list is only knowable from a live tenant. Diff it against the 31 in `x-webhooks`. Then `POST /api/{storeCode}/webhook/subscription` against a capture endpoint, configure a birthday-trigger campaign and a lapsed segment, and watch what actually arrives. LOOK FOR: any event the spec does not document, particularly anything birthday- or inactivity-shaped; what a birthday campaign firing actually emits and whether the payload carries enough to identify it without a campaign-id lookup table; whether `SegmentMembershipDropped` really is count-only; whether any per-member segment event exists that the spec omits. CONFIRMS: 31 events, two triggers missing, E2 as scoped. BREAKS FAVOURABLY IF: the tenant exposes events the vendored spec does not — in which case the vendored spec is stale and every verdict in this document needs re-reading against the live list, which is itself a finding worth having early.",
          "references": [
            {
              "id": "t5-ref-1",
              "sourceId": "SPEC",
              "filePath": "spec/openloyalty-openapi.json",
              "range": { "start": { "line": 0, "character": 0 }, "end": { "line": 0, "character": 0 } },
              "note": "Spec path paths./api/{storeCode}/webhook/event.get — the response is an untyped array of strings, so the authoritative event list is only obtainable from a running tenant. The spec cannot close this question.",
              "excerpt": "\"items\": {\"type\": \"array\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "test-t6-settings-map",
          "title": "T6 — Dump the settings map, because the expiry-notification lead time BR-P1-011 depends on is invisible to the specification",
          "summary": "STAKES: whether 'automated member notification ahead of expiry' is a configuration or a build. CALL: `GET /api/{storeCode}/settings` and `GET /api/{storeCode}/settings/choices/{type}` for each available type. LOOK FOR: the expiring-notifications lead time the vendor documentation refers to as 'X days before'; whether it is a single global value or configurable per wallet type; whether it permits more than one reminder, since BR-P1-011 says 'notification ahead of expiry' without specifying a cadence and members will likely need more than one; and what else is hiding in an untyped string map that the member surface depends on. CONFIRMS: a configurable lead time, making the expiry notification native-with-configuration as scored. BREAKS: a fixed or single-shot reminder, which pushes reminder cadence into the Notification Service and enlarges E2.",
          "references": [
            {
              "id": "t6-ref-1",
              "sourceId": "SPEC",
              "filePath": "spec/openloyalty-openapi.json",
              "range": { "start": { "line": 0, "character": 0 }, "end": { "line": 0, "character": 0 } },
              "note": "Spec path paths./api/{storeCode}/settings.get — the response schema is a bare map of strings. Every configuration decision the member surface depends on is invisible here, which is why this call has to be made rather than reasoned about.",
              "excerpt": "\"settings\": {\"type\": \"array\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"additionalProperties\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "test-t7-member-token-scope",
          "title": "T7 — Probe what a member JWT is actually allowed to do, because the Integration and Management labels are prose, not enforcement",
          "summary": "STAKES: whether the brokered architecture in E5 is mandatory or a choice. CALL: obtain a member token via `POST /api/{storeCode}/member/login_check`, then attempt `GET /member/points`, `GET /member/reward`, `GET /member/reward/bought`, `GET /member/{self}/status`, `GET /redemption?customerId={self}`, `GET /redemption?customerId={other}`, `POST /reward/{reward}/buy`, and `POST /member/{self}/reward/redeem`. LOOK FOR: which return 403; critically, whether `/redemption` with another member's `customerId` leaks — the spec's single Bearer scheme does not distinguish admin from member, so this is a real question, not a formality. CONFIRMS: Management-labelled routes reject member tokens, making brokering mandatory and E5 a stated architectural position. BREAKS: member tokens reach admin surfaces, which is a security finding for the ISO 27001 / SOC 2 evidence pack and must go to RAID immediately rather than into a design decision.",
          "references": [
            {
              "id": "t7-ref-1",
              "sourceId": "SPEC",
              "filePath": "spec/openloyalty-openapi.json",
              "range": { "start": { "line": 0, "character": 0 }, "end": { "line": 0, "character": 0 } },
              "note": "Spec block security and components.securitySchemes — a single Bearer scheme covers both admin and member JWTs, with no scopes and no per-operation security overrides except on the five unauthenticated login routes. Scope is therefore untestable from the document.",
              "excerpt": "\"security\": [{\"Bearer\": []}, {\"token\": []}] — applied globally, with no scopes declared"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "test-t8-history-actioncause",
          "title": "T8 — Read a real member history containing a reversal and an expiry, and confirm actionCause really is absent",
          "summary": "STAKES: the N+1 join in E4(c), and therefore the response-time budget for feature f4 against success metric m5. CALL: after T4, `GET /api/{storeCode}/member/{member}/history` for the affected member. LOOK FOR: whether `PointsWereCanceled` and `PointsWereExpired` appear as the spec's discriminator mapping promises; whether `flatVariables.comment` carries anything useful enough to explain the row without a join (a campaign name, a document number); whether any undocumented field appears at runtime. CONFIRMS: the join is required, and f4 needs caching or a denormalised local history. BREAKS FAVOURABLY: `comment` turns out to carry the originating document number, in which case a large part of E4(c) disappears — worth knowing, because it is the cheapest possible win in this list.",
          "references": [
            {
              "id": "t8-ref-1",
              "sourceId": "SPEC",
              "filePath": "spec/openloyalty-openapi.json",
              "range": { "start": { "line": 0, "character": 0 }, "end": { "line": 0, "character": 0 } },
              "note": "Spec path paths./api/{storeCode}/member/{member}/history.get — a well-typed discriminated event stream, which is genuinely good news for f4. The gap is causation, not structure.",
              "excerpt": "discriminator mapping includes PointsWereAdded, PointsWereSpent, PointsWereExpired, PointsWereCanceled, PointsWereBlocked, PointsWereUnlocked, RewardWasBought, CustomerWasMovedToLevel"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "test-t9-voucher-lifecycle",
          "title": "T9 — Run one voucher through its whole life and confirm expiry and invalidation behave as the schema advertises",
          "summary": "STAKES: feature f6 as promised in the brief. CALL: buy a conversion coupon with an explicit `dateValid`; read it back from `GET /api/{storeCode}/redemption/{issuedReward}`; redeem it; read it back again; attempt to redeem it a second time; then repeat with a coupon whose `activeTo` has passed. LOOK FOR: is `activeTo` set from `dateValid` as expected; does `usedAt` populate on redemption; is a second redemption refused and with what code; is an expired coupon refused at redemption or merely marked; does `CouponWillExpire` fire, and how far ahead. CONFIRMS: auto-invalidation and per-voucher expiry are native as scored, leaving only the QR as member-surface work. BREAKS: expiry is advisory rather than enforced, in which case enforcement moves to the merchant scanner and becomes tenant-facing scope.",
          "references": [
            {
              "id": "t9-ref-1",
