    {
      "id": "mock-divergences",
      "title": "Mock-vs-Spec Divergences",
      "description": "The in-repo mock at apps/mock-openloyalty is a signal of what the team already assumed, not evidence of engine behaviour. Every divergence below is a place where the team's working model and the vendored specification disagree, and where code written against the mock would be written against a contract the engine does not offer. Flagged as findings in their own right, per the brief for this activity.",
      "insights": [
        {
          "id": "mock-hides-expiry-cohorts",
          "title": "The mock hardcodes pointsExpiringNextMonth to zero and omits pointsExpiringBreakdown entirely, so nothing built against it can ever see the field feature f4 depends on",
          "summary": "⚠️ DIVERGENCE. [confidence: strong-signal — direct code inspection against direct spec inspection] `serializeCustomerStatus` in the mock returns a status object ending `pointsExpiringNextMonth: 0` with no `pointsExpiringBreakdown` key at all. The spec's `CustomerStatus` carries both. A developer building f4's expiry horizon against the mock would see a permanent zero, conclude the engine cannot express expiry cohorts, and build the shadow ledger of E3 unnecessarily — or, worse, ship a member surface that silently never warns anyone. The mock's `Transfer` interface also narrows `type` to `'adding' | 'spending'` where the engine's `UnitsTransferWasAdded` webhook enumerates `adding | spending | p2p_adding | p2p_spending | blocked | expired`, so expiry as a ledger event does not exist in the mock at all. IMPLICATION: the mock is not a safe substrate for any f4 work, and should carry a comment saying so.",
          "references": [
            {
              "id": "mockdiv-ref-1",
