              "range": { "start": { "line": 752, "character": 0 }, "end": { "line": 780, "character": 0 } },
              "note": "serializeCustomerStatus — the whole function. The zero is a placeholder that reads as a fact to anyone consuming the mock.",
              "excerpt": "pointsExpiringNextMonth: 0,"
            },
            {
              "id": "mockdiv-ref-2",
              "sourceId": "MOCK",
              "filePath": "apps/mock-openloyalty/src/data.ts",
              "range": { "start": { "line": 66, "character": 0 }, "end": { "line": 82, "character": 0 } },
              "note": "The mock's Transfer interface. expiresAt is present but optional and no expiry process ever sets or acts on it; the type union has no 'expired' member.",
              "excerpt": "type: 'adding' | 'spending';"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "mock-voucher-model-thinner",
          "title": "The mock's issued voucher has no expiry and no used-at, so BR-P1-016 and the auto-invalidation in feature f6 are invisible to anything built against it",
          "summary": "⚠️ DIVERGENCE, in the team's favour for once. [confidence: strong-signal] The mock's `IssuedReward` is `{ issuedRewardId, rewardId, customerId, couponCode, status, createdAt, statusHistory }` — no `activeFrom`, no `activeTo`, no `usedAt`, and no redeem or reissue route. The real engine's `issuedCoupon` carries all three, plus `POST .../reward/redeem` and `.../reward/reissue`, plus a `CouponWillExpire` webhook. So the engine is RICHER than the team assumed here: voucher expiry and auto-invalidation are available and do not need building. This is the one place in the desk pass where the finding reduces scope rather than adding it, and it should be told to the team as loudly as the escalations.",
          "references": [
            {
              "id": "mockdiv-ref-3",
              "sourceId": "MOCK",
              "filePath": "apps/mock-openloyalty/src/data.ts",
              "range": { "start": { "line": 114, "character": 0 }, "end": { "line": 122, "character": 0 } },
              "note": "The complete IssuedReward interface. Compare against components.schemas.issuedReward.issuedCoupon in the spec, which has three date fields this does not.",
              "excerpt": "export interface IssuedReward { issuedRewardId; rewardId; customerId; couponCode; status; createdAt; statusHistory }"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "mock-assumes-member-driven-redemption",
          "title": "The mock lets a member's own token buy a reward and read its own voucher wallet — two things the vendored spec marks Management-scoped and deprecated respectively",
          "summary": "⚠️ DIVERGENCE, and the one most likely to surprise the team late. [confidence: strong-signal for the spec labels; assumption for whether the live engine enforces them, which T7 settles] The mock mounts `POST /api/:storeCode/reward/:reward/buy` behind `requireAuth` (any authenticated caller, member included), derives the customer from the token, and serves `GET /api/:storeCode/member/reward/bought` as a live member route. In the vendored spec, `/reward/{reward}/buy` is labelled Management and its request bodies require `customerId` supplied by the caller, and `/member/reward/bought` is `deprecated: true`. The mock therefore encodes a member-driven redemption architecture that the engine's documented contract does not support — precisely the tension problem signal s5 already identified between the PWA's member-driven catalogue and the solutioning deck's staff-driven redemption journey. This divergence is not just a shape mismatch; it is the mock quietly taking a side in an unresolved design question.",
          "references": [
            {
              "id": "mockdiv-ref-4",
              "sourceId": "MOCK",
              "filePath": "apps/mock-openloyalty/src/routes/member.ts",
              "range": { "start": { "line": 165, "character": 0 }, "end": { "line": 219, "character": 0 } },
              "note": "The mock's buy handler resolves the customer from req.auth.id — a member acting for themselves. The spec's buy bodies require customerId in the payload, which is an administrator acting on a member's behalf.",
              "excerpt": "const customer = store.customers.get(req.auth!.id);"
            },
