    {
      "id": "method-caveats",
      "title": "Method Caveats — What This Pass Cannot Tell You",
      "description": "Explicit limits on the authority of everything above. Read this group before treating any verdict as settled.",
      "insights": [
        {
          "id": "caveat-not-a-spike",
          "title": "No Open Loyalty instance was called; this is a document reading and the RA-05 spike remains open",
          "summary": "[confidence: this statement is fact, not a finding] RA-05's trigger — 'access to a real Open Loyalty instance, not the in-repo mock (RAID D005)' — has not been met, and D005 remains pending. Nothing in this document was executed against an engine. An OpenAPI specification describes the shape of requests and responses; it does not describe what the system does with them. Every verdict above is therefore a prediction, and the six that carry the most weight — consumption order for expiry cohorts, partial-return proportionality, the already-spent refund case, member-token scope, whether the deprecated status endpoint still works, and the tenant's real webhook event list — are precisely the ones the specification is silent on. RA-05's status should read 'desk pass complete, spike outstanding', never 'complete'.",
